<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pentateuch, Five Books of Moses, Written Torah, Chumash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Torah is the compilation of the first five books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers and Deuteronomy, also known as the Pentateuch or the Five Books of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. Rabbinic tradition holds that its teachings were given by God through the prophet Moses, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a> and at the Tabernacle; in rabbinic literature the word also covers the Oral Torah, now embodied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> and Midrash. Biblical scholarship generally sees its final form as largely complete by the Persian period and based on earlier sources. Written on a scroll by a scribe, it is read publicly in synagogues, and it is also sacred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p></div>
